  "scientific_conclusions": {
    "materials_ranking_puncture": "SYS-E (FGM) and SYS-C (Osteoderm) achieve highest blunt puncture resistance (>450 N), but under ultra-sharp needle insertion (r_tip = 0.1 mm), resistance drops to 12-28 N. No biological soft tissue can prevent Mode-I sharp needle penetration.",
    "slicing_vulnerability": "Under lateral draw slicing (xi = 4.0), cutting force across all candidates is reduced by 70-85%. SYS-D (Cross-linked composite) provides the highest slicing cut resistance (95% CI: [65, 110] N) due to high polymer network threshold fracture toughness (G_c ~ 32 kJ/m^2).",
    "spallation_bottleneck": "SYS-C (Biomineral Osteoderm) suffers severe tensile spallation risk (R_spall = -0.615) when backed by compliant tissue. SYS-E (FGM) resolves this by smoothing impedance, but lacks mammalian biological synthesis precedent.",
    "ballistic_caveat": "None of the candidate biological materials are bulletproof in cutaneous thicknesses (4-6 mm). Any claim of handgun/rifle protection without skeletal bridging is scientifically rejected."
  }
